#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186070012 :tutorial/content "The **$y$-intercept** of a line is where the line intersects the $y$-axis. For instance, consider the line that passes through the point $(1,-1)$ and has a slope of $3$. How can we find the $y$-intercept of this line?\n\n![](images/c3/c307a60266b77cb497a5c5b02a6d9518a3b31e22b1ec97afb1d2b40f488982cb.png)\n\nWe already know values for $x$, $y$ and $m$, so we can just substitute them into the equation\n\n$$\ny=mx+b\n$$\n\nand then solve for $b$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ny &= mx + b \\\\\n(-1) &= (3)(1) + b \\\\\n- 1 &= 3 + b \\\\\nb &= -4\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the $y$-intercept is\n\n$$\n{\\color{blue}b}=-4\n$$\n\nThe coordinates of the $y$-intercept are $(0,-4)$." :tutorial/id #uuid "1f9c0e73-8a80-5472-bf82-4c78c873c181" :tutorial/math-academy-id 8059 :tutorial/title "Introduction"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186070013 :tutorial/content "The **$x$-intercept** of a line is where the line intersects the $x$-axis. For instance, consider the line $y = 2x - 4$. How can we find the $x$-intercept of this line?\n\n![](images/57/57a31eab932c948358e7ab6e428707a6a4b9f551bc956554c3a16b659b5f7988.png)\n\nAt the $x$-intercept, the $y$-coordinate is equal to $0$. So, we can substitute $y=0$ into the equation and then solve for $x$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ny &= 2x - 4 \\\\\n(0) &= 2x - 4 \\\\\n4 &= 2x \\\\\n2 &= x \\\\\nx &= 2\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the $x$**-intercept** is $x=2$. The coordinates of the $x$-intercept are $(2,0)$." :tutorial/id #uuid "fbd0b44d-8460-5058-9b94-c21852d09a4b" :tutorial/math-academy-id 8060 :tutorial/title "Finding the X-Intercept of a Straight Line"}]}
